======================================================================
THE MULTIPLE COMPARISONS PROBLEM
======================================================================
Total comparisons made: 10 (t-tests) + 10 (Fisher's exact) = 20
At alpha=0.05, we expect ~1.00 false positives by chance alone, even if none of the true underlying differences existed.
Some of the 'significant' results found in Steps 4-5 are therefore likely to be noise, not real differences — correction is needed before acting on them.
Note: Bonferroni and FDR below are each applied separately within their own test family (10 t-tests, 10 Fisher's exact tests) — that's why the correction counts are out of 10 per family, not out of the combined 20. Each family is a distinct set of null hypotheses, so correcting them jointly would conflate two different questions (CPA vs. conversion rate).

