======================================================================
KEY INSIGHT
======================================================================
t-test family: 0 of 10 CPA comparisons were significant before correction — CPA does not differ meaningfully by Campaign_Type in this dataset at all; every Cohen's d was negligible (|d| < 0.02, see ttest_comparisons output). This is a genuine null result, not a false positive that correction needs to catch.
Fisher's exact family: 10 of 10 conversion-rate comparisons looked significant before correction; 9 survive Bonferroni and 10 survive FDR — because clicks are aggregated in the millions per group, even a ~0.1-0.9% relative difference clears the corrected p-value bar. Every one of these differences is far below the 10% relative-difference practical-meaningfulness screen used in fisher_exact_tests.py — this is the same 'statistically significant but not practically significant' pattern as the CPA family would have shown if CPA had any real variation to detect.
